  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nA cold front has moved through, resulting in well below normal\ntemperatures today with highs near or below 80 for the\nnorthwestern half of the Quad State, near cold maximum records.\nStorms have not fired in the eastern portions of the coverage \narea, and at this point are not expected to. Wrap-around drizzle\nor light rain showers are occurring as a cutoff low pressure\ncenter drifts eastward across Central Illinois. Light \nprecipitation coverage will be highest in the northeast through \ntomorrow. A cold air funnel might be possible during the \nafternoon today or tomorrow, but will likely require some degree\nof ambition from these showers.\n\nHighs in the upper 70s to near 80 are forecast areawide Sunday,\nagain near record low maximums, with drizzle to light showers \nalong with lingering low cloud ceilings keeping things dreary. A\nfew lightning flashes might be possible tomorrow afternoon in \nan environment with limited CAPE and shear, though thunder \nchances were lowered compared to the initialization. The cutoff \nlow very slowly continues its eastward drift through Central \ninto Eastern Kentucky early next week. PoPs lower and become \nmore diurnally driven early next week. Some model variation in \npositioning of the low as it drifts early next week has kept \nthunder in the forecast but, should the system progress towards \nEastern Kentucky, that aspect will likely be removed narrowed if\nnot completely removed.\n\nDry conditions are forecast Wednesday with light winds. Clearing\nalong with the cessation of northerly flow will allow for\ntemperatures to begin climbing, continuing to warm later in the\nweek as southerly flow returns. A cold front reaches the Great\nLakes region from the Northern Plains Thursday, but high \npressure becomes entrenched in the southeast and the associated \ncold front may not reach the Quad State. Models are divided as \nto whether showers and storms are able to develop late week, \nwith slight chance-chance PoPs included at this time. Increased \nmoisture along with highs reaching 90 results in 100-105 degree \nheat indices Friday to Saturday.",
